# ENCY extension cookbook — rules that apply to every entry point

Whatever kind of extension you write, these four topics decide whether it is stable. Each section is
the short version; the upstream doc it links to is the long one.

## 1. COM lifetime — `ComWrapper<T>`

Every object you get from the ENCY API is a COM object living in the host process. Hold one raw and you
pin ENCY objects and block your own library from unloading.

```csharp
using var appCom = ComWrapper.Create(context.CamApplication);   // takes ownership; null-safe
if (appCom.IsNull) throw new Exception("No application");

// method with no COM result (or a plain .NET result)
appCom.Invoke(app => app.SomeVoidCall());
string exe = appCom.Invoke(app => app.ExecutablePath);

// method returning another COM object -> wrapped, ownership transferred
using var techCom = appCom.InvokeAndWrap(app => app.Technologist);

// method returning (result, TResultStatus) -> throws on rsError, no manual check
string id = techCom.Invoke(t => (t.CreateOperation("TSTFaceMillingOp", prevId, "", out var s), s));
```

- `ComWrapper.Create(x)` is the shorthand for `new ComWrapper<T>(x)`; both accept null and give you an
  empty wrapper with `IsNull == true`.
- `ListComWrapper<T>` is the same idea for collections and **takes ownership on `Add`** — do not
  dispose what you added.
- One owner per object. If you hand a COM object to a window that will outlive the call, do **not**
  wrap it in `using` — let the window's `Dispose()` be the single release point.

Long version: `cam-api-examples/docs/general/com-lifetime.md`.

## 2. Errors — `TResultStatus`, never exceptions

```csharp
resultStatus = default;
try
{
    // work
}
catch (Exception e)
{
    resultStatus.Code = TResultStatusCode.rsError;
    resultStatus.Description = e.Message;   // the user reads this
}
```

- **No exception may cross the COM boundary.** Every entry-point method catches everything and reports
  through its status (`TResultStatus`, or `IPLMResult` for PLM).
- **Check the status of calls that hand you one.** Many API methods report through `out TResultStatus`
  or `ref TExecuteContext` instead of throwing; an ignored `rsError` reads as success and corrupts the
  next step. `ComWrapper`'s status-aware `Invoke`/`InvokeAndWrap` overloads throw for you.
- **Non-fatal conditions go to the log, not the status:** `context.Logger?.Log(TLogEventType.leWarning,
  "MyPlugin", "No geometry selected")` — `IExtensionLogger` writes to the ENCY log window. For
  something the user must notice, `UIDialogs.Notify(TLogEventType.leInfo, "MyPlugin", "Done")`.
- **A user cancelling a dialog is not an error** — return the default status.

Long version: `cam-api-examples/docs/general/error-handling.md`.

## 3. Asking the user for parameters

Prefer the **inspector dialog** (`CamApiInspectorWindow` + `SimplePropIterator` from
`CAMAPI.UIDialogs`): a native ENCY-style property grid, no WPF, no XAML, no threading rules to obey. It
looks like the rest of ENCY, which is what users expect.

Reach for your own window only when the input genuinely needs a custom UI (charts, previews, trees).

Long version: `cam-api-examples/docs/general/ui-patterns.md` (Pattern 1).

## 4. Windows — STA, COM and unloading

ENCY's .NET bridge runs **MTA**; WPF/WinForms need **STA**. Two cases:

**Modal (the call waits for the answer).** Create a dedicated STA thread, `Join` it, capture the result
through a closure:

```csharp
var names = GetOperationNames(context);        // read COM data HERE, on the MTA thread
string? picked = null;
var t = new Thread(() => { var w = new PickWindow(names); if (w.ShowDialog() == true) picked = w.Selected; });
t.SetApartmentState(ApartmentState.STA);
t.Start();
t.Join();
```

**Critical:** do not touch COM objects inside the STA lambda — read what you need before, pass plain
.NET values in.

**Non-modal (the window outlives the call).** The library must not be unloaded while it is on screen:
implement `IExtensionLazyUnloadable` and keep `CanUnload == false` until the window closes. Easiest
correct version is `ExtensionWindowLazyUnloadable` (from `CAMAPI.DotnetHelper`), which owns the HWND,
the `WindowHelper.ShowStaWindow` call and the `CanUnload` bookkeeping:

```csharp
public class MyExtension : IExtension, IExtensionUtility, IExtensionLazyUnloadable
{
    private readonly ExtensionWindowLazyUnloadable _windowManager = new();
    public IExtensionInfo? Info { get; set; }
    public bool CanUnload { get => _windowManager.CanUnload; set { } }
}
```

`WindowHelper.ShowStaWindow(ownerHandle, createWindow, onClosed, onException)` starts the STA thread,
runs a dispatcher, disposes the window on close and routes STA-thread exceptions to your handler.

Classic ways to crash the host:
- `using` on a COM object handed to a long-lived window (disposed when `Run` returns → dead reference);
- skipping `IExtensionLazyUnloadable`, or reporting `CanUnload = true` while the window is open;
- calling COM from the UI thread through `.Instance` instead of `ComWrapper.Invoke`;
- disposing the same COM object both in `Run` and in the window's `Dispose()`.

Long version: `cam-api-examples/docs/general/non-modal-window.md` and `ui-patterns.md` (Patterns 2-3).

